<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayapura</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hollandia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayapura is the capital of the Indonesian province of Papua, on Yos Sudarso Bay on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1910 as Hollandia, it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/netherlands-new-guinea">Netherlands New Guinea</a> and in 1944, after its capture from the Japanese, served as General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a>'s headquarters. It received its present name in the 1960s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jayapura.jpg" alt="Jayapura" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nekomong · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kota_Jayapura,_Papua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jayapura lies on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, on Yos Sudarso Bay, and covers 835.48 km2. The land varies from valleys to hills, plateaus and mountains up to 700 metres, and about 30% of the area is inhabited, the rest being rough terrain, swamp and protected forest. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> to the east, and has a tropical rainforest climate with heavy rain throughout the year. The name is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for city of victory and was given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> as part of the removal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>'s name from the city. Some people have proposed local names such as Port Numbay, after the Numbay river, whose name means clear water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial government the place was known as Numbay. Trade there was active, centred on the island of Metui Debi, and was carried on by barter for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, salted fish and bird-of-paradise plumes. The society of Numbay, or Tabi in general, was led by an ondoafi, the chief of the tribe. By 1849 the region was a tributary of the Tidore Sultanate. On 28 September 1909 a Dutch naval detachment under Captain F.J.P. Sachse came ashore at Humboldt Bay to explore northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and look for a natural border between the Dutch and German spheres. Their camp, Kloofkamp, gives its name to an old district of the city.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> flag was raised on 7 March 1910 and the settlement named Hollandia. Across the bay was a German camp, Germania-Huk. The Dutch made Hollandia the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/netherlands-new-guinea">Netherlands New Guinea</a> in 1945. Japanese forces occupied the north of the territory in 1942, and Allied forces drove them out after landings near Hollandia from 21 April 1944. Naval Base Hollandia then served as General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a>'s headquarters until the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in March 1945, with more than twenty U.S. bases and half a million U.S. personnel passing through the area.</p><p>After the territory passed to the United Nations Temporary Executive Authority on 1 October 1962, the city had the dual name Hollandia and Kota Baru. Indonesia took control on 1 May 1963, and the city was briefly called Sukarnopura before it received its present name at the end of 1968. In 1949 the Roman Catholic Church had established the Apostolic Prefecture of Hollandia, which since 1973 has been the Diocese of Jayapura.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jayapura is the capital and largest city of the province of Papua. It was estimated to have 410,852 inhabitants in mid 2024, which makes it the second most populous city on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a>. As the provincial capital it has drawn migrants from other regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and its football club Persipura has won the national league four times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jayapura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fort, renamed Fort <b>Hollandia</b>, survives as a ruin at Princes Town.</blockquote><a href="/state/brandenburger-gold-coast">Brandenburger Gold Coast</a></li><li><blockquote>Governed from <b>Hollandia</b>, the colony saw Dutch investment in schools, missions and administration aimed at preparing Papuans for self-rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/netherlands-new-guinea">Netherlands New Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
